let __v_34 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pineapple\",\"pineapples\",\"pineapple juice\"]},\"Fine. Pineapple does not bring on labour: the enzyme people blame for it is in the stem, not in the fruit you eat, and a portion has nowhere near enough of it to do anything. So have it fresh, tinned or juiced - the myth is the only thing to worry about.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"juice\",\"juices\",\"orange juice\",\"apple juice\",\"fruit juice\",\"grapefruit juice\",\"cranberry juice\",\"tomato juice\",\"a glass of juice\"]},\"Juice is fine: the cartons and bottles in a shop are pasteurised already, so nothing on the pregnancy list applies to them, and juice you squeeze yourself is fine if you wash the fruit and drink it the same day. It is mostly sugar without the fibre that slows it down, so one glass with a meal is the habit to aim for rather than sipping it through the day, and whole fruit is the better version of the same vitamins.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mango\",\"mangoes\",\"mangos\",\"melon\",\"melons\",\"watermelon\",\"grapes\",\"grape\",\"kiwi\",\"kiwis\",\"berries\",\"strawberries\",\"blueberries\",\"raspberries\",\"blackberries\",\"peach\",\"peaches\",\"pear\",\"pears\",\"plum\",\"plums\",\"banana\",\"bananas\",\"orange\",\"oranges\",\"apple\",\"apples\",\"pineapple chunks\",\"satsuma\",\"satsumas\",\"clementine\",\"clementines\"]},\"Fine, and worth eating: fruit is where most of the vitamin C comes from. Rinse it under the tap first, because listeria and toxoplasmosis are on the outside of fruit and veg rather than in it, and a melon wants washing before it is cut, not after. Fruit juice is fine too, but it is sugary - whole fruit is the better habit.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"papaya\",\"papayas\",\"pawpaw\",\"pawpaws\",\"green papaya\",\"unripe papaya\"]},\"Ripe papaya is fine. The one to leave is the unripe green papaya, and papaya skin and seeds: unripe papaya carries a latex-like substance that is said to act on the womb, so a green papaya salad is one to skip for nine months. A ripe orange one is a good breakfast.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"honey\"]},\"Fine for you. The warning about honey is for babies under one, whose gut can let botulism spores grow - it does not apply to you or to the baby before birth. It is still sugar, so a spoon in a drink rather than a jar a day.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"hummus\",\"houmous\",\"humous\",\"tahini\",\"houmus\"]},\"Fine, including the tubs from the supermarket: shop hummus is made with pasteurised sesame and chickpeas, and sesame is not one of the allergens to avoid in pregnancy. Keep an opened tub in the fridge and eat it within a couple of days, because it is a cold food and the listeria advice is about cold food.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"spicy food\",\"spicy\",\"curry\",\"curries\",\"chilli\",\"chili\",\"chillies\",\"chilies\",\"hot sauce\",\"spices\",\"spice\"]},\"Fine. Spicy food does not bring on labour or harm the baby, and the old advice to avoid it was about the mother's comfort. If you get heartburn, which is common later on, it is worth eating smaller amounts more often rather than avoiding spice altogether.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ice cream\",\"icecream\",\"soft serve\",\"soft-serve\",\"soft whip\",\"milkshake\",\"milkshakes\"]},\"Fine from a tub: ice cream is made with pasteurised milk. The one to skip is soft-serve from a machine, the sort that sits in a hopper all day - that is the cold-served food listeria has been found in, and a machine that is not cleaned often is the risk. A scoop from a shop tub is no riskier than a supermarket one.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"coleslaw\",\"slaw\",\"potato salad\",\"deli salad\"]},\"Fine from a packet or a supermarket tub, which is made with pasteurised mayonnaise. The one to skip is salad that has been sitting out on a deli counter, and anything you are unsure has been kept cold: listeria grows in food that is kept cold for a while, not in food that is freshly made.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bean sprouts\",\"beansprouts\",\"sprouts\",\"sprouted seeds\",\"alfalfa\"]},\"Cook them through, or leave them: raw bean sprouts and sprouted seeds are one of the foods listeria and salmonella have been found in, and washing does not reliably clear them. Sprouts cooked into a stir fry, a curry or a soup are fine.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"olives\",\"pickles\",\"gherkins\",\"sauerkraut\"]},\"Fine, and the vinegar or brine they are in is not a problem. They are salty, so if your ankles or blood pressure are being watched, keep them to a few rather than a bowl.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"dates\",\"dates fruit\"]},\"Fine, and a good snack: they are sweet, so they are the thing to reach for instead of a biscuit at four in the afternoon. There is no evidence they bring on labour, whatever you read.\"]}]}")?;
