let __v_82 = Value::from("A test is the answer, and the timing is the part people get wrong: from the day your period is due, on the first pee of the morning, which has the most hormone in it. A faint second line is still a line. If it is negative and nothing comes in three days, test again, because the hormone doubles every couple of days. Early signs - sore breasts, going off food, tiredness, feeling sick - are not reliable enough to go on either way. If you would rather not be pregnant, a GP or a sexual health clinic will go through the options without pushing you either way, and the sooner you ring the more there is to choose from.");
frame.store("TEST_REPLY", __v_82.clone())?;
let __v_83 = Value::from("Ring the GP surgery and say you have had a positive test: the booking-in appointment is usually between 8 and 10 weeks, and they will sort the midwife, the scans and the bloods. Start 400 micrograms of folic acid and 10 micrograms of vitamin D every day, and stop alcohol - most of what you eat is still fine, and the list of what is not is short. If the line was faint or early, test again in three days. A negative test with no period in three days is worth testing again; a positive one with pain or bleeding is a call to the GP or 111 rather than something to sit on.");
frame.store("POSITIVE_REPLY", __v_83.clone())?;
let __v_84 = Value::from("A negative test this early is worth repeating rather than believing: test again in three days, first pee of the morning, because the hormone doubles every couple of days and an early test can simply be too soon. Plenty else shifts a period - stress, a bug, a bad month of sleep, a change in weight, travel, the pill - so one late period with a negative test is common and usually settles. Ring the GP if nothing comes for three months, if the tests stay negative but you feel pregnant, or if there is pain one side at all.");
frame.store("NEGATIVE_REPLY", __v_84.clone())?;
let __v_85 = Value::from("A period that comes early is common and usually just a short cycle: stress, a bug, a change in sleep or travel, a big drop in weight, or the pill can all bring one on, and the spotting some people get at ovulation a couple of weeks before is often taken for one. Write the dates down for the next two or three - if they stay under three weeks apart, if you bleed between periods or after sex, or if the bleeding is very heavy, that is a GP appointment. If there is any chance you could be pregnant, test anyway: a bleed can happen early on.");
frame.store("EARLY_REPLY", __v_85.clone())?;
let __v_86 = Value::from("The usual cycle is 21 to 35 days from the first day of one period to the first day of the next, and the bleeding itself is usually two to seven days - up to eight is still normal. What is worth a GP appointment: cycles closer than 21 days or longer than 35, bleeding that lasts more than eight days, bleeding between periods or after sex, soaking a pad or tampon an hour for several hours, or three months with no period at all. Write the dates down for three cycles first, because that is what the GP will ask for, and the pill can change the pattern while you are on it.");
frame.store("CYCLE_REPLY", __v_86.clone())?;
let __v_87 = Value::from("Plenty shifts a period by a few days - stress, a bug, a bad month of sleep, a change in weight, travel, the pill - so the useful next step is a test rather than a wait: from the day it was due, first pee of the morning, and again in three days if it is negative and nothing comes. Three months without a period, bleeding between periods or after sex, or a positive test with pain or bleeding is a GP appointment. If your cycles were always irregular, a few weeks late on its own is not alarming.");
frame.store("LATE_REPLY", __v_87.clone())?;
