let __v_1392 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"c section\",\"c-section\",\"caesarean\",\"caesarian\",\"cesarean\",\"cesarian\",\"caesarean section\",\"cesarean section\"]},\"A caesarean is the baby delivered through a cut in the tummy and womb, usually because labour is not progressing, the baby is breech or either of you is unwell. It is a major operation: about 45 minutes, a spinal so you are awake, and roughly four to six weeks of careful lifting afterwards. An emergency one is done for a reason found in labour, not because anything went wrong on your part.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"doula\",\"doulas\"]},\"A doula is a person who supports you through pregnancy, birth and the first weeks - practical help and someone who stays with you - but she is not a midwife and does no clinical care. Doulas are hired privately, usually GBP 300 to 800, and there are free volunteer doulas in many areas; ask what she does at a hospital birth, as some trusts limit how many people are in the room.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"midwife\",\"midwives\"]},\"A midwife is the trained professional who looks after a straightforward pregnancy, birth and the first weeks, and she is who you see at the antenatal appointments. She is a nurse or a direct-entry midwife with a degree, and she can order scans and blood tests, deliver the baby and stitch a tear. A doctor takes over only if something needs one.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"epidural\",\"epidurals\"]},\"An epidural is pain relief given through a fine tube in the back, numbing from the waist down while you stay awake. It takes about 20 minutes to set up and 15 to 20 to work, and it can make your legs heavy and slow labour, so it is usually offered once labour is established. You can still push with one; ask early if you want one, because it needs an anaesthetist.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"birth plan\",\"birth plans\"]},\"A birth plan is a page saying what you would prefer - who is with you, how you want pain relief, water, feeding, and what to do if things change - written so a midwife can read it in a minute. Keep it to one side of A4 and put your first two wishes at the top: the plan that helps is a short list of preferences, not a script, because birth often goes its own way.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"induced\",\"induction\",\"inductions\",\"being induced\",\"induced labour\"]},\"Induction is starting labour with medicine or a balloon instead of waiting for it to start itself, usually offered if you go past about 41 weeks or there is a reason the baby is better out. It is a prostaglandin tablet or gel first, then a drip if needed, and it can take two or three days from the first dose to the baby. You can ask what the reason is and what happens if you wait.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"water birth\",\"water births\",\"birthing pool\",\"birth pool\"]},\"A water birth is labouring, and sometimes delivering, in a deep pool of warm water, which takes the weight off and often eases the pain of contractions. Many birth centres and some labour wards have pools; ask early, as there may be one and it may be taken. You can get out at any point, and being in water is not offered if there are complications.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"colostrum\",\"colostrum harvesting\"]},\"Colostrum is the first milk: thick, yellow and made in small amounts in the last weeks and the first days, and it is all a newborn needs - a teaspoon at a time, full of antibodies. It acts as a laxative that clears the meconium, which is why the first dirty nappies come. Hand expressing a little into a syringe before the birth is worth asking about if your baby may need extra.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"membrane sweep\",\"stretch and sweep\",\"sweep\",\"sweeps\"]},\"A membrane sweep is a midwife's gloved finger passing over the cervix to separate the membranes, which releases hormones and can start labour - offered from about 40 weeks if you are keen to avoid induction. It takes a minute, feels like a smear test and a period cramp, and works for roughly one in eight. You can say no and ask again in a week.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ventouse\",\"ventouse delivery\",\"vacuum delivery\",\"vacuum extraction\"]},\"A ventouse is a soft cup on the baby's head that a doctor pulls with, to help in the last stage when pushing is not moving the baby or either of you is tiring. It leaves a bruise and a cone-shaped head that settles in a few days. Forceps do the same job with metal tongs; the doctor will explain which they want and why.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"episiotomy\",\"episiotomies\"]},\"An episiotomy is a small cut made at the bottom of the vagina during birth to make room, done with local anaesthetic when the baby needs to come quickly or the tissue is about to tear badly. It is stitched and heals in two to three weeks, and it is not routine - a tear that happens on its own is often smaller and heals better, so not every birth needs one.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"nicu\",\"scbu\",\"special care baby unit\",\"neonatal unit\"]},\"NICU is the neonatal intensive care unit, and SCBU the special care baby unit - where a baby goes if they are early, small or need help breathing or feeding. You can visit almost all the time, ask to do the nappy changes and feeds, and ask the nurse to explain every tube and number: babies on these units are usually there for warmth, feeding and time.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"labour\",\"in labour\",\"going into labour\"]},\"Labour is the womb contracting to open the cervix and push the baby out: for a first baby it usually takes 8 to 18 hours from regular contractions, and it comes in three stages - the cervix opening to 10 cm, the pushing, and the placenta. Call the midwife when contractions are regular, getting longer and stronger, about every five minutes.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"placenta\",\"the afterbirth\",\"afterbirth\"]},\"The placenta is the organ on the womb wall that feeds the baby through the cord, and it comes out as the third stage of labour, usually within 15 to 30 minutes of the birth. You can have an injection to speed that up or wait for it to happen on its own. It is checked whole afterwards, which is the one reason to look at it if you want to.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"birth centre\",\"birth centres\",\"midwife led unit\",\"midwifery led unit\"]},\"A birth centre is a midwife-led unit for a straightforward birth - a room with a pool, your own space, no doctors on site and usually a shorter stay, so it suits a pregnancy with no complications. If something needs a doctor you are transferred to the labour ward, which is usually minutes away. Ask for a look round at about 30 weeks.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"home birth\",\"home births\",\"birth at home\"]},\"A home birth means two midwives come to you, with gas and air and a pool if you have one, and you are transferred to hospital by ambulance if it is needed. It is offered for a straightforward second or later pregnancy, and it is your choice to ask for it - about 2 in 100 births in the UK happen at home.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"perineal tear\",\"tear\",\"tears\",\"second degree tear\",\"third degree tear\",\"a tear\"]},\"A tear is the skin at the bottom of the vagina stretching and splitting as the baby comes: most are small, most need a few stitches with local anaesthetic, and they heal in two to three weeks. A third or fourth degree tear reaches the muscle and the back passage and is repaired in theatre. Tell your midwife about the pain rather than sitting on it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lochia\",\"postnatal bleeding\",\"bleeding after birth\"]},\"Lochia is the bleeding after birth: heavy red blood for three to five days, then browner and lighter for up to six weeks. Use pads rather than tampons, and get help the same day if you soak a pad in an hour, pass clots bigger than a 50p coin, or the blood smells bad or you feel feverish - that is a sign to have checked.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"heel prick test\",\"newborn blood spot\",\"blood spot test\",\"heel prick\"]},\"The heel prick test is the newborn blood spot test, done at five days old: a few drops of blood from the heel onto a card, checked for nine rare conditions including cystic fibrosis, sickle cell and an underactive thyroid. It takes a minute and the baby cries briefly. It is offered to every baby and you can ask what each result means.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"mucus plug\",\"bloody show\",\"show in pregnancy\",\"a show in pregnancy\",\"show\"]},\"The mucus plug is the plug of mucus that seals the cervix, and it comes away as a thick jelly, sometimes streaked with blood - the 'show'. It can be days or a fortnight before labour and means nothing on its own; call the midwife if the blood is more than a streak or comes with regular contractions.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"nuchal scan\",\"nuchal translucency\",\"dating scan\",\"20 week scan\",\"anomaly scan\"]},\"A nuchal scan is the 12-week scan: it measures the fluid at the back of the baby's neck and, with a blood test, gives a risk figure for Down's, Edwards' and Patau's syndromes - a screening test, not a diagnosis. The 20-week scan checks how the baby is growing and developing. Both are offered, and you can decline or ask for time to think.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"skin to skin\",\"skin to skin contact\",\"kangaroo care\"]},\"Skin to skin is the naked baby laid on your bare chest, covered with a blanket, in the first hour and as often as you like after. It keeps them warm, steadies their breathing and blood sugar, and is the strongest start to feeding - and it works just as well for a dad or a partner, which is useful when the mother needs stitching or a rest.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"forceps\",\"forceps delivery\",\"kiwi cup\"]},\"Forceps are the metal tongs a doctor uses to guide the baby out in the last stage, when pushing is not moving them or one of you is tiring. They leave marks on the head that fade in a few days, and you are given pain relief first. They are used in about one birth in fourteen, and a ventouse is the alternative when a cup is enough.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tongue tie\",\"tongue-tie\",\"tongue tied\",\"tongue-tied\"]},\"A tongue tie is the strip of tissue under the tongue being tight or short, so the tongue cannot lift or reach forward as far as it should - which can make feeding sore and slow. It is common, it is checked at the newborn examination, and a simple snip in the first weeks is quick and needs no anaesthetic. Ask a midwife or health visitor to look if feeding hurts.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"gestational diabetes\",\"gestational diabetes test\",\"glucose tolerance test\"]},\"Gestational diabetes is high blood sugar that starts in pregnancy, from the placenta's hormones making insulin work less well - it is tested for with a glucose drink and blood tests at 24 to 28 weeks if you are at risk. It is managed with diet, monitoring and sometimes tablets or insulin, and it usually goes after the birth. Nothing you did caused it.\"]}]}")?;
